T3 Code on OpenRC
=================

This package installs an OpenRC adapter, not T3 Code. Portage owns
/etc/init.d/t3code, /etc/conf.d/t3code and /usr/libexec/t3code-openrc.
Upstream owns the T3 executable, its runtime versions and updates. The
service account owns its state, database and logs. Installing this package
does not download, initialize, enable, start or restart anything.

Before replacing an existing service
------------------------------------

A custom /usr/local/libexec/t3code-service or other local wrapper stays a
separate file; this package neither deletes nor replaces it. Review the old
init script and configuration, back up the service state and database, and
plan an explicit service stop before changing a live deployment. Do not run
two supervisors against the same T3 home.

Portage config-protect may keep an existing /etc/init.d/t3code or
/etc/conf.d/t3code and install a protected update next to it. Merge those
changes deliberately (dispatch-conf or etc-update). Installing this package
does not switch a custom service to this adapter, and nothing restarts.

Protocol-2 Node runtimes and copied launchers are incompatible. Changing the
protocol number does not migrate them: "initialize" refuses any existing
state, even malformed JSON, directories and dangling symlinks. Existing
deployments need a separate, backed-up migration of runtime and state.

Install the runtime as the service user
---------------------------------------

Pick an existing non-root account with an absolute home directory and log in
as it. The steps below use curl (net-misc/curl), which this package only
depends on with USE=tailscale; install it first if you lack it. The upstream
installer is a manual user action, never a package hook. Read the script
before running it:

  curl --fail --location --output ./t3-install.sh https://t3.codes/install.sh
  less ./t3-install.sh
  t3_home="$HOME/.t3"   # choose the runtime home once and reuse it below
  T3CODE_CHANNEL=nightly T3CODE_HOME="$t3_home" sh ./t3-install.sh
  "$HOME/.local/bin/t3" --version

Do not run "t3 service install" on OpenRC: upstream's service-manager
integration expects systemd. The installer does not create OpenRC state.

Copy the exact installed SemVer from the version output, in upstream's
spelling (including any -nightly and build suffix, no leading "v"). Do not
use the Gentoo _pre spelling or the dist-tag "nightly". Then initialize the
downloaded runtime, with t3_home set to the same absolute path you will put
in t3code_home:

  version='REPLACE_WITH_EXACT_INSTALLED_SEMVER'
  T3CODE_HOME="$t3_home" /usr/libexec/t3code-openrc initialize "$version"
  T3CODE_HOME="$t3_home" /usr/libexec/t3code-openrc check

"initialize" needs a non-root uid and writes private state only if none
exists; "check" only reads. Neither downloads or runs T3.

Configure and enable
--------------------

As administrator, set the account and runtime home in /etc/conf.d/t3code
(quote paths containing spaces). t3code_home must be the exact path used for
the installer and "initialize" above:

  t3code_user="YOUR_EXISTING_ACCOUNT"
  t3code_home="/ABSOLUTE/PATH/TO/THAT/ACCOUNTS/.t3"

The service binds 127.0.0.1 on t3code_port (default 3773) and gets HOME from
the account's passwd entry. Codex, Claude or OpenCode helpers must be
installed for that account; the adapter does not install them. After
reviewing any config-protect updates, enable and start it yourself:

  rc-update add t3code default
  rc-service t3code start
  rc-service t3code status

A missing or incomplete runtime is a startup failure; the service never
bootstraps it. Upstream handles runtime updates and rollback. Updating the
desktop Portage package does not update this user runtime. The service log
is userdata/logs/openrc.log under t3code_home.

Optional Tailscale Serve
------------------------

Off by default; the disabled path needs neither the Tailscale package nor a
running daemon. To use it, build with USE=tailscale, configure and
authenticate tailscaled separately, and check "tailscale serve status"
first. Reserve a dedicated, otherwise unused HTTPS port for this adapter,
do not share it with another service, and do not let T3 manage Serve
itself. Set all three options, with the target port matching t3code_port:

  t3code_tailscale="true"
  t3code_tailscale_https_port="9443"
  t3code_tailscale_target="http://127.0.0.1:3773"

OpenRC creates the mapping after start and removes only that port on stop.
If a post-start check fails, inspect the service status and stop the service
before retrying, and review any mapping an interrupted start left behind. To
change the port while running, stop with the old configuration first.

Verify the deployment
---------------------

Check the environment descriptor, not a guessed /health path (the web app
answers HTTP 200 for unknown paths). It must show the installed version and
capabilities.serverSelfUpdate == "boot-service":

  port=3773   # your t3code_port
  curl --fail --silent --noproxy '*' \
    "http://127.0.0.1:${port}/.well-known/t3/environment" | jq .

Inspect process ownership and the log above. With Tailscale, read back
"tailscale serve status" and probe the advertised HTTPS host and port, then
confirm the local and HTTPS versions agree; a mapping alone does not prove
remote reachability. After one approved restart, repeat the checks to
confirm the setup survives it.
